<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tariq ibn Ziyad</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 720 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tariq ibn Ziyad was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> commander in the service of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate</a> who led the Muslim invasion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberian Peninsula</a> in 711 CE. After crossing from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> he defeated the Visigothic king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roderic">Roderic</a>, opening the way to the conquest of most of Iberia. The rock of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gibraltar">Gibraltar</a> takes its name from him, and accounts of the Umayyad <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">Caliphate</a> discuss him in its account of the empire's western conquests.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tariq-ibn-ziyad.jpg" alt="Tariq ibn Ziyad" width="187" height="243" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tariq_ibn_Ziyad.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
